{"id":"d355637c-a2ad-4069-b23e-347457fa40cb","object":"chat.completion","created":1784988254,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"import copy\\nfrom collections import deque\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    # Validate required fields\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'utility_threshold' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required field: nodes or utility_threshold\\\")\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"nodes must be a list\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"utility_threshold must be numeric\\\")\\n\\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"node id must be string\\\")\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            if parent not in children_map:\\n                children_map[parent] = []\\n            children_map[parent].append(nid)\\n        # ensure each node has entry in children_map\\n        if nid not in children_map:\\n            children_map[nid] = []\\n\\n    removed = set()\\n    visit_order = []\\n    in_queue = set()\\n\\n    # initial queue: all nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    all_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    queue = deque()\\n    for n in all_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in in_queue:\\n            in_queue.add(nid)\\n            queue.append(nid)\\n\\n    while queue:\\n        nid = queue.popleft()\\n        in_queue.discard(nid)\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        # check if leaf (no children currently)\\n        is_leaf = len(children_map.get(nid, [])) == 0\\n        if is_leaf and node.get('removable', False) and node['utility'] <= threshold:\\n            # remove this node\\n            removed.add(nid)\\n            # update parent's children list\\n            parent = node.get('parent')\\n            if parent is not None and parent in children_map:\\n                if nid in children_map[parent]:\\n                    children_map[parent].remove(nid)\\n                # enqueue parent for reconsideration if not removed\\n                if parent not in removed and parent not in in_queue:\\n                    in_queue.add(parent)\\n                    queue.append(parent)\\n\\n    # retained nodes in original order\\n    retained = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained,\\n        \\\"visit_order\\\": visit_order\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python code for `solve(case)` that implements the described algorithm.\n\nFrom the problem description: we have a tree of nodes, each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also a utility_threshold.\n\nThe algorithm: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Skip nodes already removed. Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold. After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\"\n\nWe need to output `retained_node_ids` (list of node IDs that remain after removals) and `visit_order` (list of node IDs in order of visitation? likely the order we process nodes in the queue).\n\nInterpretation: We have a queue of nodes to consider for removal. Initially, we need to put nodes into the queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order (presumably ascending ID for same depth? \"descending depth and stable node-ID order\" likely means first by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (stable means deterministic). Actually \"stable node-ID order\" might mean order by node ID (stable could mean the order as given? But the IDs are strings). Most straightforward: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending.\n\nThen we process the queue: pop node, if it is already removed, skip. If it is not a leaf? The description says \"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" So we only remove if the node is a leaf (no children in the current retained tree) and removable=True and utility <= threshold. If conditions met, we remove it (mark as removed) and then enqueue its parent for reconsideration (if parent is not null and parent is still retained). We also add the node to visit_order? The output says `visit_order` - likely the order in which nodes are visited (i.e., popped from queue and considered). So every time we pop a node from queue, we append its id to visit_order, regardless of whether it is removed or not? Or only when we attempt to remove? The problem states \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" The visit order should be the order in which we process nodes from the queue. So we should record each node when we pop it from the queue.\n\nWe need to maintain the tree structure dynamically as nodes are removed. Initially, we have all nodes. For each node, we can maintain a set of current children (from the original parent relationships). When a node is removed, we need to remove it from its parent's children list, and also we should not consider it as a child of its parent for leaf detection.\n\nAlso \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So when we pop a node that is marked removed, we skip (don't add to visit_order? Probably still add to visit_order to indicate we visited it but it was already removed? The description says \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" The visit order likely includes all nodes that are considered for removal (including those skipped because already removed). Since the algorithm says \"Skip nodes already removed.\" it implies they are still encountered in the queue. So we should add them to visit_order but not process further.\n\nAdditionally, after removal, we enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. We must ensure we don't enqueue a parent that is already removed. Also we need to avoid duplicate entries in the queue? We can either use a set to track already queued or just allow duplicates and later skip removed ones. Since we skip removed nodes, duplicates of already removed nodes are harmless but may cause extra work. To be safe, we can use a set to avoid enqueuing a node that is already in the queue. Or we can simply enqueue parent and later check if it's already removed.\n\nThe algorithm continues until queue is empty.\n\nFinally, after processing, we need to collect retained_node_ids: all nodes that are not removed. We can collect them from the original nodes list, filtered by not removed.\n\nThe output schema: JSON object with keys \"retained_node_ids\" (array of strings) and \"visit_order\" (array of strings). Order of retained_node_ids? Not specified, but likely stable (e.g., sorted by original order? Or as they appear in the input?). We need to preserve some order. The problem says \"Retain IDs\" - we can just list them in the order they appear in the input? But the input nodes are not in any particular order. We can sort by id for consistency. However, the algorithm might depend on the initial ordering. But the \"retained_node_ids\" is just a set of IDs; order likely doesn't matter. But to be safe, we can return them in the same order as they appear in the original nodes array (filtered). However, the output specification doesn't enforce order for retained_node_ids. We'll return in the order they appear in the original nodes list (preserving input order). That seems reasonable.\n\nWe need to use only standard library. No randomness. Use deterministic sorting.\n\nWe'll implement:\n\n- Build a map from node id to node info, and also a map from parent to children list (initially based on parent field). We'll maintain a set of removed node ids.\n- Initialize queue: sort nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending. We'll push them into a list (or use collections.deque). We'll pop from the front? Typically queue FIFO. The description says \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" That is the initial order. Then \"After removal enqueue its retained parent\". That suggests we add to the end of the queue (enqueue). So we use a deque, pop from left, enqueue to right. We'll also maintain a set `in_queue` to avoid enqueuing same node multiple times.\n\n- While queue not empty:\n    node_id = queue.popleft()\n    add node_id to visit_order\n    if node_id in removed: continue\n    node = node_map[node_id]\n    # check if leaf: no children in current children map (key present but empty list)\n    # also check removable and utility <= threshold\n    if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= utility_threshold and not children_map.get(node_id, []):\n        # remove it\n        removed.add(node_id)\n        # remove from parent's children list\n        parent_id = node['parent']\n        if parent_id is not None and parent_id in children_map:\n            children_map[parent_id].remove(node_id)\n        # enqueue parent if parent not removed and parent exists\n        if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\n            if parent_id not in in_queue:\n                in_queue.add(parent_id)\n                queue.append(parent_id)\n\n- After loop: retained = [node['id'] for node in nodes_list if node['id'] not in removed] (preserving original order)\n\nReturn {\"retained_node_ids\": retained, \"visit_order\": visit_order}\n\nWe must handle that nodes may not have children list initially; we build children_map from all nodes (except root? root parent null). Also note that when we remove a leaf, we also need to update the leaf status for parent. The parent might become a leaf after removal. That's why we enqueue parent.\n\nWe must be careful: if a node has multiple children and one is removed, it's no longer a leaf; we only enqueue parent; later when we process parent, it may still have other children, so it won't be removed unless it becomes leaf later. That's fine.\n\nAlso, initial queue should include all nodes? The description: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" So initially we enqueue all nodes? Probably yes, because we need to consider each node for removal. But if a node is not a leaf initially, we will still pop it, check conditions, fail (since not leaf), and then do nothing else. That's fine.\n\nBut we also need to ensure that we process nodes that become leaves later after their children are removed. Those are enqueued when child removed.\n\nOne nuance: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" When we pop a node that was removed (either because it was removed earlier, or because it was a child that was removed and later its parent was enqueued), we skip it. That's handled.\n\nWe need to convert utility to float for comparison.\n\nEdge cases: If root is removable? The fixtures show root not removable. But if it were, we should not remove it? The algorithm says \"Remove only a removable leaf\". So root could be leaf if no children, but if root is not removable, it won't be removed. Also even if removable, root has no parent so no enqueue.\n\nAlso, utility_threshold may be a float; compare with <=.\n\nNow, about `_case_nonce`: ignore, just part of payload.\n\nWe must output exactly the implementation function. The submission should be a JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code as a string.\n\nThe code should define `solve(case)` that returns a dict with keys \"retained_node_ids\" and \"visit_order\". It should not mutate case; we will copy necessary fields.\n\nWe'll write code using only standard lib (collections.deque, maybe copy).\n\nWe'll include error handling: if required fields missing, raise ValueError. But we assume input is as per fixture.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON output.\n\nWe need to ensure the Python source is properly escaped.\n\nWrite the code in a clean manner.\n\nLet me produce the answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2133,"completion_tokens":2651,"total_tokens":4784,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1979},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":85},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}